# Static Prototyping

- Expect the user to attach a PRD and base the prototype on its requirements.
- Save prototypes in `03-static-prototyping/`.
- Make each prototype a single self-contained HTML file. Embed images and JavaScript inline.
- A use case may have multiple prototype variants so the user can compare different approaches.
- Make the HTML aesthetically pleasing and easy to scan while keeping it structural only and greyscale. Use a clean sans-serif system font stack (for example, `Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif`); do not use Times New Roman.
- Use consistent spacing, alignment, and a clear text hierarchy to create a polished layout. Keep visual elements to plain boxes, borders, and text labels; do not use color or decorative styling.
- Do not use chart libraries, canvas, SVG rendering, or animation.
- Render every element, including charts, as a plain bordered box with a text label describing what it will become. Nothing should be a working chart.
- Do not document prototype decisions.
